<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Does Washington state have an income tax?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Washington state does not have a personal income tax on wages, salaries, or other earned income. This makes Washington one of the best states for take-home pay, alongside Texas, Florida, Nevada, Wyoming, South Dakota, Alaska, Tennessee, and New Hampshire."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the WA Cares Fund and how much does it cost?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The WA Cares Fund is Washington's long-term care insurance program. Employees pay a premium of 0.58% of gross wages with no cap. This funds a lifetime benefit of up to $36,500 for long-term care services."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What taxes do Washington workers actually pay?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Washington workers pay federal income tax (10-37%), Social Security tax (6.2% up to $176,100), Medicare tax (1.45%), WA Cares Fund (0.58%), and Paid Family/Medical Leave (~0.54% employee share). There is no state income tax."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does Washington have a capital gains tax?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Washington imposes a 7% tax on capital gains exceeding $250,000 from stocks, bonds, and other capital assets. This does not apply to real estate or retirement distributions."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does Washington compare to other no-income-tax states?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Washington is one of nine states with no personal income tax. Compared to Texas, Washington has lower property taxes but higher sales tax. Compared to Florida, Washington has higher wages in tech but higher cost of living."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Paid Family and Medical Leave deducted from my Washington paycheck?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Washington PFML has a total premium of 0.74% of gross wages in 2025. Employees pay about 72.76% of that, approximately 0.54% of wages."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does Washington have high sales tax instead of income tax?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Washington relies on sales tax as primary revenue. The state rate is 6.5% with local additions bringing combined rates to 8.5%-10.5% in most areas. Seattle combined rate is 10.25%. Groceries are exempt."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much does the average Washington worker save compared to California?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A worker earning $150,000 saves approximately $12,000-$14,000 per year in state income taxes by living in Washington instead of California."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I opt out of the WA Cares Fund?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The initial opt-out window closed December 31, 2022. New exemptions are limited to veterans with 70%+ service-connected disabilities, certain non-immigrant visa holders, and workers living outside Washington."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is this Washington payroll calculator free?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. This calculator is completely free with no registration, no email, and no data collection. All calculations happen in your browser for instant, private results."}}]}</script>
